A||||19||Gokart||FIRES!?!||||5||
Z||000000||Gokart||12-09-1999||08:41 PM||||I hear talk sometimes about Fieros being notorious for catching fire. Where and how would one start? Oil leaks on hot exhaust parts? TIA for replys.||||reg||5
Z||000001||87GT||12-09-1999||08:47 PM||||That is why Pontiac recalled all its 84 model year Fieros.  Supposedly all the 84s got bad rods that would break and puncture the engine block causing all the contents to leak onto the exhaust components causing a fire.  Every Iron Duke in 84 was replaced in I think 1988 or 1989.  1984 was the year of the fire problems.  I found some articles through search engines about the fiero fires. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cooter||12-09-1999||08:47 PM||||They way a Fiero catches fire was explained to me this way:<BR>IF the cooling fan fails, IF the engine overheats, IF you keep driving in this OVERHEATED condition, IF the oil breaks down to the point of no longer providing lubrication, a connecting rod MIGHT sieze to the crankshaft and COULD POSSIBLY break and ventilate the forward side of the block and COULD spill overheated oil onto a overheated cat. converted and MIGHT catch on fire.<P>A lot of IF's and MIGHTS in the description- just dont run the car hot and use good oil and there should not be any problems.||||reg||13
Z||000003||Trixi||12-09-1999||08:48 PM||||i don't know if it is true or not but i herd the wire harness on the 84 because of it's location will burn||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||12-09-1999||10:53 PM||||Don't forget, the 4-cyl Fiero engines naturally ran a quart low on oil. Then, when the level was allowed to go even lower by neglectant owners, you got a extra crispy Fiero.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 12-10-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Shiner||12-10-1999||12:13 AM||||Trixi,<BR> Mine hasn't caught fire yet, but that wiring harness DOES melt on the '84's.  I know of one person who's harness DID melt, and DID start a fire.  Be sure to check it out everytime you open the trunk, '84 owners.  The story on the '84's that I heard was that the fiero plant was being supplied with bad rods from another GM factory.  Supposedly, up to 60% of these rods were not up to standards.  With the duke (before the recall) already being low on oil... well.. you get the picture.  It was actually a very small amount of cars that actually caught fire.  If properly maintaned, you should not have any problems.  It's funny; a few dozen fieros catch fire, and the public and press labels them a "deathtrap", meanwhile, THOUSANDS of different Fords burn to the ground.....  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||ChadMan||12-10-1999||08:59 AM||||When I bought my 84 in 92, I went to the library and did some research on Fiero fires.  I don't remember the source, but I remember reading that fewer than 400 fires had occurred in Fieros at that time - not too many when you consider 330K Fieros were made.<P>Still, I always carry an extinguisher behind the driver's seat just in case.  So far, all I've had to extinguish was some kid's frog-crap green Olds 98 that had a carb fire on I-270 in Columbus!||||reg||1
Z||000007||dogtired||12-10-1999||12:53 PM||||2 Words:<BR>Maintenance, MaintEnance, MAINTENANCE.<P>Check and make sure your RECALLS have been done. Check your oil every time you fill up with gas. Check on top of your starter, leaves and road trash accumulate there and is a great source to burn? Check your wire harnesses and feel (make damn sure) confortable they are not too close to the exhaust. Then make sure your maintenance is kept up to date. Better to be cautious than be flammable   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>As for all the FORDS that burnt, I think that was the only way the owners could get rid of them?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||fierospeeder||12-10-1999||01:15 PM||||My friend whose has a lot of experience about cars, doesn't think the fiero can start on fire if the oil gets on the exhaust.  I had a leaky trans on my tbird and the fluid would hit my downpipe and all it would do was smoke.  <BR>My 84 fiero has that heat insulator around the ecm harness and the previous owners done that recall thing.  The lady who owned the car, 2nd owner, clipped all these newspaper articles of the car starting on fire, some of the articles were written in the 90s.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Richard Parnell||12-10-1999||01:27 PM||||It seems this is my pet answer to a number of Fiero problems.  Yes, the infamous oxygen sensor.  On my Fiero, I had the O sensor missing when I first bought it.  The engines timing went so far out as a result that the exhaust became cherry red. I mean so hot you could literally see through the manifold.  When I looked in the engine compartment the hot manifold was making the "fireproof" insulation smoke.  I think a number of cars probably burned this way.  It's a theory.||||reg||12
Z||000010||Richard Parnell||12-10-1999||01:27 PM||||It seems this is my pet answer to a number of Fiero problems.  Yes, the infamous oxygen sensor.  On my Fiero, I had the O sensor missing when I first bought it.  The engines timing went so far out as a result that the exhaust became cherry red. I mean so hot you could literally see through the manifold.  When I looked in the engine compartment the hot manifold was making the "fireproof" insulation smoke.  I think a number of cars probably burned this way.  It's a theory.||||reg||12
Z||000011||WKDFIRO||12-10-1999||02:55 PM||||Another possible, probable, only if the planets are out of alignment and the ozone layer is at a certain point, chance for fire. On the V6.<P>Just below the battery tray holding on to the battery's plastic divider is a sheet metal screw just above the fuel lines. If for some innane reason the fuel lines get loose or are just close enough with the engine's vibration the point of the screw could in some rare cases can put a hole in it. The former President of our club had his gorgeous car burn and we think thats where it came from. Just take the point off of the <BR>screw for piece of mind. <P>When it comes to engine fires nothing should be taken for granted. I totally agree with Maintence x3!<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 12-10-1999).]||||reg||4
Z||000012||Standard||12-10-1999||07:23 PM||||1 in 4 Fiero's had the bad rods.  Pontiac got rather pissed at the supplier over that one...||||reg||11
Z||000013||william_thorniii||12-10-1999||07:50 PM||||To all concerned with wires burning up:<P>A simple and essentially free of detering this is to wrap them in aluminum foil.  Sure it looks a little strange, but if it does reduce the chance of melting my harness, I am all for it.  <P>To those with stereos:<P>It also works well for + lines going to the battery if you are planning on running them through the manual shift cable hole into the cabin.  This is what I did and have not experienced any alternator interference to this day.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000014||Monkeyman||12-10-1999||08:52 PM||||dogtired--That's either one word or 3, depending on how you look at it.  Just thought you might want to know. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I guess your name fits.  You aren't a truck driver, are you?  I know, when I was one, I tended to get a little tired.  Just trying to figure out your handle.||||reg||1
Z||000015||2birds||12-10-1999||11:08 PM||||I had to replace a shifter cable back in '89, because it was laying on the head pipe and burned through.  While I was doing this, I noticed the wiring harness for the AC compressor was also laying there, and the insulation had become quite crispy.  Just one more spot to check, especially after changing the forward bank of plugs.  And one more reason to work on it yourself. <p>[This message has been edited by 2birds (edited 12-10-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||batboy||12-10-1999||11:43 PM||||Fierospeeder, if you get enough engine oil or tranny fluid onto the exhaust, it can cause a fire. True, I've seen some old beaters with major oil leaks running down onto the manifolds and they never seem to catch on fire, but why take the risk. The engine compartment of a Fiero runs a tad warmer than a conventional front engine car, which adds to the risk.||||reg||1
Z||000017||dogtired||12-12-1999||01:37 AM||||Another side note on burning  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><BR>A good friends throttle cable got against the exhaust and cooked the cable. Didn't catch on fire but did leave him stranded.<BR>Monkeyman - No never did drive a truck, do drive Fieros, Corvetts, 57 Chevies, assorted others and my family Crazy.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>||||reg||6
Z||000018||Monkeyman||12-12-1999||03:39 AM||||dogtired--I don't ever drive my family crazy.  Especially not my wife.  (Although she tends to have a slightly different opinion! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)||||reg||1
Z||000019||lews84||12-12-1999||10:05 PM||||A few years ago, I was getting my Fiero ready for emission testing (seeing if it would start). After letting it warm up I looked in the rear view mirror and saw flames coming out of the engine compartment. I bailed out with my fire extinguisher as I hit the trunk release.<P>By the time I got back there the fire was almost out. I have a bad habit of letting my Fiero sit under a tree for long periods of time and a large number of leaves had collected on and around the exhaust. The leaves were the only thing that burned. Fortunately leaves don't burn long or hot, but it sure got my attention. Now I have a car cover for it.<P>Recently I had a LOT of smoke coming from the engine compartment. Oil was leaking from the valve cover (I assume), running down the engine and dripping onto the catalyst shield. Using instructions I got from Batboy, theogre and others I fixed the valve cover leak. Most of the smoke has disappeared but I don't know  for sure because I found out the brakes were shot.<P>Now my Fiero is on jacks while I replace the brakes. Seems sitting for long periods doesn't do them any good. Again thanks to instructions I got here, I finally used vise grips and a number of penetrating oils and got the brake hose disconnected from the brake line. Once it 'popped' it came off easily.||||reg||11
